            <div xml:id="d689">

                <pb facs="0977" n="861" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg861"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">637.0023/102: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Cuba</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Howell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-10-09T12:00:00-05:29"><hi rendition="#italic">October 9,
                                1923—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received 4:44 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>125. My 117, October 4, 2 p.m., and 124, October 8, 4 p.m.<anchor n="32"
                        xml:id="d689-f1"/> The President signed the Tarafa Bill this morning. He is
                    expected to <pb facs="0978" n="862" xml:id="pg862"/> write a letter in answer to
                    Mr. Crosby’s protest. The letter will give his interpretation upon certain
                    passages of the Bill which are not clear. Mr. Crosby states such a letter would
                    make the interests he represents more satisfied with the law.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Howell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
